sweet-wlan-irq-doorbell (lowclk 2026-11-13; design notes: lowclk irq study as corrected by study-check; write table: impl-writes): the Wi-Fi INTx doorbell "stage A" and a read-only logging mode, both behind boot-arg sweetwifiirq.
sweetwifiirq absent or 0: nothing new runs, no register is read or written, no log line (an invalid value logs a GAP and counts as 0). Needs the keep thread (sweetwlankeep=1, sweetwlan=26); without it the boot-arg is ignored with a GAP.
sweetwifiirq=2 (read-only): once a second in the keep thread, with the link up and the dongle awake (the gate: engine not stopped, no soft stop, d4sleep 0, bus not dead, the sw3_g2_ok link check), READ ELBI+0x000, ELBI+0x010 and BAR0 PCIe core +0xC30 and print
`SWEET WLAN IRQ: ro elbi0=<x> en0=<x> c30=<x> rings_work=<0|1>` (rings_work = the drain of the same pass found items). Answers whether ELBI IRQ0 bit 14 is a latch or follows the line (study U-A). The judge table has no write row in this mode.
sweetwifiirq=1 (stage A): the corrected enable order of study-check s.4, in the keep thread under the en0 lock: 0xC34 := 0, ack 0xC30 until zero (W1C of the bits read, drain between), ELBI IRQ0 W1C bit 14 only, GIC attach INTID 541 with the SPI DISABLED (patch 0346),
ELBI IRQ0_EN |= bit 14 (read back), SPI enable, 0xC34 := 0xFFFF last (read back). ISR (interrupt context, ELBI only): read IRQ0, no bit 14 -> count `spurious` and mask; bit 14 -> W1C bit 14 only, set pending, wake the keep thread, return SWEET_SPI_DEFER (the GIC masks the level line).
Thread (every pass, the 5 ms / 100 us poll is unchanged and stays the fallback): on a pending interrupt ack 0xC30 FIRST, then drain rings 2/3/4, re-read 0xC30 and loop (<= 8), then sweet_spi_unmask. Counters irqs / missed (the poll pass found ring work and no interrupt fired) / spurious / storm.
Auto-off (back to pure polling, loud GAP, teardown): > 5000 ISR entries in a second (storm), 200 poll-found-work passes with zero interrupts ever, 3 ISR entries in a row without bit 14, 0xC30 stuck, any ISR ELBI error or judge refusal, SPI unmask failure. Teardown order (every step tried): ELBI IRQ0_EN bit 14 off, SPI detach, 0xC34 back to its value before arming (engine end: no BAR0, the engine's undo writes 0xC34 := 0).
One summary line per 5 s: `SWEET WLAN IRQ: irqs N missed N spurious N storm N mode <ro|doorbell|off:<why>>`.
Bus judges: the new header's own table (sweet_wlan_irq.h swi_rows[] + swi_value_ok: exact offsets, access types, value rules, per mode) is checked on EVERY access, including the ISR's; BAR0 stores additionally pass the engine's g2l[] judge, which gets three rows ONLY when sweetwifiirq=1
(0xC34 := 0, 0xC34 := 0xFFFF, kind SW_G2K_MBX_ACK for 0xC30 W1C). The old review rule F9 (0xC34 unmask refused while an RC enable is set) is rewritten (sw3_g2_irq_off): with the doorbell armed, IRQ0_EN may read exactly bit 14 and IRQ1_EN/IRQ2_EN must read 0; otherwise unchanged.
Files: new bsd/dev/sweet_wlan_irq.h (pure core between SWI_CORE_BEGIN/END, host test tools/test_wlan_irq_core.py), bsd/dev/sweet_wlan.c (judge rows, rewritten F9 rule, kernel accessors, keep thread hooks). Needs patch 0346 (sweet_spi_attach_off / enable / unmask / SWEET_SPI_DEFER). ORDER: after 0346, 0313.
NOT RUN, NOT BUILT: the kernel glue has been syntax-checked with the real compile flags only; no register of this patch has ever been touched on hardware. The new write path is UNREVIEWED (AGENTS rule 8: it needs its one review before it boots).
